<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. P. Morgan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as John Pierpont Morgan, Pierpont Morgan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Pierpont Morgan was an American financier and investment banker who dominated corporate finance on Wall Street in the Gilded Age and Progressive Era. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hartford-connecticut">Hartford, Connecticut</a>, in 1837, he worked for his father's banking houses and headed the firm that later became JPMorgan Chase &amp; Co. He drove a wave of industrial consolidations, forming U.S. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a>, International Harvester and General Electric, and his firm held controlling interests in businesses such as Western Union and 21 railroads. During the Panic of 1907 he organised a coalition of financiers that saved the American monetary system from collapse. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1913, leaving his business to his son, J. P. Morgan Jr.</p></div>
